# bench/labels/dast-juiceshop.truth - the HAND-AUTHORED ground truth for the
# B7 DAST leg, against the local, operator-owned OWASP Juice Shop test target
# (bench/corpus.lock's `dast-juiceshop` row; tools/dast-test-target.sh starts
# it, per docs/DAST-TEST-TARGET-AUTHORIZATION.md).
#
# WHY THIS TRUTH SET IS SMALLER AND NARROWER THAN THE OTHER B6 LABEL FILES,
# STATED UP FRONT.  Juice Shop is a client-rendered Angular SPA: its server
# returns almost no links for a static crawler to follow, so both tools here
# see the SAME small, fixed surface - the handful of static assets/routes a
# plain HTTP walk reaches plus a short, published-knowledge list of its own
# REST endpoints (the ones this file's own cases probe below) - rather than
# the whole application. See this leg's own bench/results/b7-dast-juiceshop/
# README.md for how each tool's crawl/spider actually did against this same
# ceiling, including a ZAP AJAX-spider attempt that could not be measured at
# all in this environment (that README, not this file, carries that account).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# METHODOLOGY RULE R2, APPLIED HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No tool's output of any kind was consulted to produce a single row below.
# Every case is either (a) an HTTP fact obtained by probing the target
# DIRECTLY with `curl -i` (never scoursh, never ZAP) and reading the raw
# response, or (b) a live behaviour reproduced BY HAND against the target's
# own HTTP API with a documented public payload for a challenge OWASP Juice
# Shop itself ships and names (its own `data/static/challenges.yml`
# publishes "Login Admin" - bypass the admin login without knowing the
# password - as a first-tier SQL Injection challenge; this file's one `real:
# true` sqli case is that exact challenge, reproduced directly).  This is the
# same category of source B6's terragoat-aws.truth used (reading the
# Terraform, never a tool's report on it) and kubernetes-goat.truth used
# (the scenario's own walkthrough) - a target's own published behaviour or
# documentation, not a scanner's opinion of it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE UNIT IS ONE (ENDPOINT PATH, VULNERABILITY CLASS) PAIR
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DAST has no file/line to anchor a case to (AGENTS.md's fingerprint table:
# a DAST finding's location is `target method path_template param_location
# param_name`, never a byte range), so `--match file` (the default
# granularity; see bench/README.md's own section on this) is used throughout
# and `file` here is a URL PATH - scheme, host and query stripped - exactly
# what bench/tools/scoursh-dast.sh's own `path_template` mapping and
# bench/tools/zap.sh's own `urlsplit(...).path` both normalise a finding's
# `file` field to. Two DIFFERENT vulnerability classes on the SAME path are
# two cases (this file has several); two cases of the SAME class scored
# against the SAME path would double-count one observation as two, which is
# why no category below repeats a path.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FOUR CATEGORIES, AND WHAT EACH ONE CAN AND CANNOT SHOW
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `cors` and `missing-csp` are UNIFORM ACROSS THIS TARGET - Juice Shop applies
# the same permissive CORS policy and omits Content-Security-Policy on every
# response this file probed, with no clean counter-example anywhere on the
# app to use as a negative control. That is disclosed rather than hidden:
# both categories can measure RECALL here (does the tool report the
# misconfiguration where it genuinely exists) but cannot measure a
# false-positive rate from this corpus alone - bench/score.sh's own `rate()`
# renders TN/FP as `0/0` -> `n/a` rather than a fabricated 0.000, and
# bench/README.md's own "both directions of false accounting" section is the
# place this limitation is stated for the published table too.  `sqli` and
# `info-disclosure` are the categories that DO carry negative controls, for
# the opposite reason: SQLi is not uniform (one hand-verified real bypass,
# several endpoints that take no attacker-controlled value into a query at
# all), and no response probed here discloses an engine/framework banner, so
# every `info-disclosure` case is a trap by construction.
#
# `missing-csp` is ONE CASE, not one per endpoint, because scoursh's own
# `DAST-HDR-CSP_MISSING-01` is a per-APPLICATION check (one finding, at the
# first endpoint in a fixed order - AGENTS.md's DAST-05 section, "One finding
# per check per target") - matching that grain here is what keeps a tool's
# score from depending on how many of the 13 assets it happened to crawl.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY OUT OF SCOPE FOR THIS FILE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# scoursh's own DAST engine ships 92 checks (docs/COMPARISON.md's DAST row) -
# auth/session handling, JWT algorithm confusion, GraphQL introspection,
# rate-limiting, host-header injection, object-level authorization, and more
# - and scoursh's own passive run against this same target found a real,
# high-confidence `DAST-JWT-SIG_NOT_VERIFIED-01`.  None of that is scored
# here: this file covers only what could be hand-verified against the RAW
# target, independent of any tool, inside this ticket's time budget.  That is
# a scope limit on the CORPUS, not a claim about what either tool can find -
# see bench/README.md's "lead each category with the scope cell" framing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT: bench/lib/truth.sh records - five 0x1f-separated fields, no sixth
# (this corpus has no line ranges to score - see "the unit" above).
#
#     <case><US><file><US><category><US><cwe><US><real>
#
# 20 cases: 14 real, 6 negative controls.

# ==========================================================================
# cors - Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * observed on every response probed
# (CWE-942, permissive cross-origin resource sharing)
# ==========================================================================

# cors-root  (GET /)
#   The SPA shell document. curl -i confirmed "Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *"
#   with no credentialed-request handling alongside it - a static wildcard,
#   not origin reflection.
cors-root/cors942true

# cors-robots  (GET /robots.txt)
#   Same wildcard header, confirmed directly; a "public" static file is
#   exactly the case a wildcard is defensible for, which is why this corpus
#   also probes routes that plainly are not (below).
cors-robots/robots.txtcors942true

# cors-styles  (GET /styles.css)
#   Same wildcard header on a bundled asset, confirmed directly.
cors-styles/styles.csscors942true

# cors-search  (GET /rest/products/search)
#   A REST endpoint invisible to a static crawler (no <a href> reaches it)
#   but directly reachable and, per curl -i, carrying the identical wildcard
#   header - this and the five rows below it are the cases that actually test
#   whether a tool discovered the SPA's API surface at all.
cors-search/rest/products/searchcors942true

# cors-whoami  (GET /rest/user/whoami)
#   Confirmed directly; returns JSON identity data behind the same wildcard
#   CORS policy.
cors-whoami/rest/user/whoamicors942true

# cors-appversion  (GET /rest/admin/application-version)
#   An admin-prefixed but unauthenticated (200, not 401) endpoint - confirmed
#   directly, same wildcard header.
cors-appversion/rest/admin/application-versioncors942true

# cors-languages  (GET /rest/languages)
#   Confirmed directly, same wildcard header.
cors-languages/rest/languagescors942true

# cors-continuecode  (GET /rest/continue-code)
#   Confirmed directly, same wildcard header.
cors-continuecode/rest/continue-codecors942true

# cors-captcha  (GET /rest/captcha)
#   Confirmed directly, same wildcard header.
cors-captcha/rest/captchacors942true

# cors-challenges  (GET /api/Challenges)
#   The loopback ORM REST layer (distinct from /rest/*), confirmed directly
#   with the same wildcard header - a 65 KB JSON document naming every
#   challenge in the app, itself the source this file's own sqli-login case
#   below points at for the exact payload it reproduces.
cors-challenges/api/Challengescors942true

# cors-apiusers  (GET /api/Users)
#   401 Unauthorized (no session) - but curl -i shows the CORS header is set
#   on the 401 response too, so the misconfiguration is present regardless of
#   auth state. Confirmed directly.
cors-apiusers/api/Userscors942true

# cors-ftp  (GET /ftp)
#   A legacy static file-listing route, confirmed directly, same wildcard
#   header.
cors-ftp/ftpcors942true

# ==========================================================================
# missing-csp - no Content-Security-Policy header anywhere on the target
# (CWE-1021, missing framing/script-origin control)
# ==========================================================================

# csp-root  (GET /, ONE case for the whole application)
#   curl -i against / (and, independently, against every other endpoint
#   probed for the cors cases above) shows no Content-Security-Policy header
#   anywhere on this target. Scored as a single app-level case at the root
#   document, matching scoursh's own DAST-HDR-CSP_MISSING-01 grain (one
#   finding per application, not per endpoint - AGENTS.md, DAST-05) rather
#   than inflating it into 13 near-duplicate cases.
csp-root/missing-csp1021true

# ==========================================================================
# sqli - SQL injection (CWE-89)
# ==========================================================================

# sqli-login  (POST /rest/user/login, real:true)
#   OWASP Juice Shop names this challenge explicitly - "Login Admin", tier 1,
#   SQL Injection - in its own shipped challenge catalogue. Reproduced by
#   hand, directly against the target, with no scanner involved:
#     curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3400/rest/user/login \
#       -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
#       -d '{"email":"admin@juice-sh.op'"' --","password":"x"}'
#   returned HTTP 200 with a valid JWT for the admin account
#   (role":"admin") despite an arbitrary password - the classic
#   comment-out-the-password-check bypass. Confirmed working at the time this
#   file was written; a real, hand-verified positive.
sqli-login/rest/user/loginsqli89true

# sqli-languages-clean  (GET /rest/languages, real:false)
#   Takes no request body and no query parameter that reaches a database
#   query - it serves a static language-list JSON document. A finding here
#   would be a false positive by construction.
sqli-languages-clean/rest/languagessqli89false

# sqli-captcha-clean  (GET /rest/captcha, real:false)
#   Generates and returns a CAPTCHA; takes no attacker-supplied value used in
#   a query. Same reasoning as sqli-languages-clean.
sqli-captcha-clean/rest/captchasqli89false

# ==========================================================================
# info-disclosure - engine/framework version banner (CWE-200)
# ==========================================================================

# ALL FOUR CASES ARE NEGATIVE CONTROLS.  curl -i against every endpoint this
# file probes shows no X-Powered-By, no Server: Express, and no other
# version-naming header anywhere on this target - confirmed directly, and
# independently consistent with scoursh's own passive banner check reporting
# zero disclosures on this same target (that consistency is NOT how this
# call was made; it is noted only because a reader will otherwise wonder).
# A tool that reports an engine/framework disclosure on any of these four is
# a false positive.
infodisc-root-clean/info-disclosure200false

infodisc-robots-clean/robots.txtinfo-disclosure200false

infodisc-languages-clean/rest/languagesinfo-disclosure200false

infodisc-appversion-clean/rest/admin/application-versioninfo-disclosure200false
